          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does \"with\" require the two terms to sit next to each other in the documentation, or is it an index convention?",
          "answer": "Based on the retrieved passages, \"with\" is an index convention, not a requirement that the two terms sit next to each other in the record.\n\nThe ICD-10-CM coding guidance says that where the Alphabetic Index links two conditions with the word \"with,\" the classification reads this as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the relationship. It does not require the record to state the link (ICD-10-CM code structure and conventions; the type 2 diabetes coding summary describes the same rule). Under this convention, a record listing type 2 diabetes and chronic kidney disease is coded as diabetic kidney disease unless the documentation says the two are unrelated.\n\nThe same passages add two limits. The presumption holds only for the pairings the index actually lists. It is also overridden when the documentation says the two conditions are unrelated.\n\nThe passages do not say how the index is laid out, whether the two terms must appear in a particular order, or how a coder decides when a record is ambiguous. For those details, you would need the Alphabetic Index itself or a coding professional.\n\nThis is general information about how a coding system works. It is not a statement about any particular person's health record.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
